            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Shutterbug111"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This is awesome! I too (as someone else had written) thought this was something only my family has called \"pink stuff\". It must be a southern thing? \nI only used maybe half of the condensed milk and 3/4 of a can of strawberry pie filling. I found it to not be too sweet at all! In fact, I added marshmellows and a dash or two of cinnamon! Everyone loved it and I like how easy it is to make and to \"tinker\" with you're own flavors."
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Tink"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This also known in my family as \"Pink Stuff\"! But, it has some additional ingredients: an 8 oz. package of softened cream cheese, 1/3 cup of lemon juice beat together, then fold in the other ingredients listed with the exception of only 1/2 cup chopped pecans. Oh, and we freeze it! Defrost enough to scoop out, or put in paper liners in muffin cups, freeze then unmold and store in a plastic bag, taking out only what you need. My nephew was the one who named it \"Pink Stuff\" as in, Aunt Denise, did you bring any of that Pink Stuff with you? Haha!! It was known as Frozen Fruit Salad before that...",
            "datePublished": "2012-12-23T07:25:42.153Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Nadelle"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I love this recipe! My recipe calls for \n1/4 cup lemon juice and mandarin oranges. This might be why others think it's too sweet. I also\nprepare a graham cracker crust..and bake it for a few minutes in a 9x13x2 pan.",
            "datePublished": "2008-04-21T01:25:30.913Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Shirley Spidle Hedge"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I added tablespoon lemon juice, drained mandarin oranges and marshmallows. Then I froze it in cupcake liners. So great!",
            "datePublished": "2025-05-19T16:45:17.092Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "MERRI C"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Also always knew this as \"pink stuff\"! We use a can of whole cranberries instead of the cherry. Delicious! I love that someone mentioned portioning out in muffin tins and freezing. We always froze ours but in a 13x9 pan. Just make sure to let it set out 8-10 minutes before cutting in to portions if doing in a 13x9 pan.",
            "datePublished": "2026-06-15T23:58:27.253Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "symtan"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I make mine with Kroger Extra Cherry Pie filling, Oregon Pitted Dark Cherries, drained and Luxardo Maraschino Cherries, drained. When I really want to be extra I throw in a tub of Philadelphia Original Whipped Cream Cheese Spread. In the summer I like to pour the mix into a shortbread crust (I don't like Graham Cracker) and then freeze it. Drizzle with Chocolate syrup before serving.",
            "datePublished": "2026-03-30T15:53:45.372Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "passion8chef"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This recipe is delicious as written. I combined the pie filling, drained crushed pineapple, condensed milk and nuts the night before and let them chill overnight. Several hours before my event I folded in the whipped topping and returned to refrigerator. Everyone loved it!",
            "datePublished": "2019-09-12T01:02:12.570Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Robin Keller"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Delicious! I poured the can of cherry filling onto a cutting board and chopped it into little pieces which made the consistency wonderful. Everyone at Thanksgiving loved this. I made half with nuts and the rest w/o for non-nut lovers. So easy to make. Thanks for the recipe. Oh - I took out some calories by using low fat condensed milk, lite pie filling and lite cool whip. No one even knew it was a lite recipe :)",
            "datePublished": "2009-12-04T16:27:45.273Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "rebecca"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Surprised I could find the “pink stuff” recipe online! We use a can of dark sweet cherries (drained), dark cherry jello, cottage cheese, marshmallows and whipped cream container. Looks the same! Great to see the variations!",
            "datePublished": "2022-11-17T16:12:35.172Z"
          },
          {
